Put N data points in Rp as the rows of a matrix and subtract the mean of each column, giving a centred N×p matrix X. The sample covariance matrix is C=1N−1XTX. If X=UΣVT then C=VΣTΣN−1VT, so:
the principal directions are the right singular vectors vi (eigenvectors of C);
the variance along vi is σi2/(N−1);
projecting the data onto v1,…,vk is the best k-dimensional summary in the least-squares sense (Eckart–Young applied to X).
Worked example. The four points (3,3),(−3,−3),(1,−1),(−1,1) already have mean zero. XTX=(20161620) has eigenvalues 36 and 4 with eigenvectors (1,1) and (1,−1), so the singular values of X are 6 and 2. The first principal direction is 12(1,1) with variance 36/3=12; the second has variance 4/3. The first component explains 36/(36+4)=90% of the total variance.
Common pitfall: Forgetting to centre the data. Without subtracting the mean, the first "principal component" mostly points at the mean itself. Also scale the columns first when they are in different units, or the largest-unit variable dominates.
